{#
  Xray server configuration for VLESS over WebSocket behind TLS.

  Same two non-obvious settings as the first transport, for the same reasons:
  `log.access: none` (Xray's access log is a record of who connected to what, and
  that record must not exist) and `sniffing.enabled: false` (sniffing extracts the
  destination hostname from a client's traffic — we neither route on it nor want
  to learn it).

  One more that is specific here: `alpn` is `http/1.1` only. WebSocket does not
  run over HTTP/2 in this setup, and offering h2 in the handshake advertises a
  capability the endpoint does not actually serve — a mismatch an active prober
  can measure.

  One more thing is load-bearing, and it is the whole of M3: the OUTBOUND. This node
  originates every connection a user makes, so where that connection is opened FROM is
  where the user appears to be. On an entry node with a hop there is exactly one
  outbound and it is bound to the hop's near address (`sendThrough`) — so there is no
  outbound left that could reach the internet by this node's own route, and when the hop
  is down the connection cannot be opened at all. Decision 3, fail closed, as a property
  of the file rather than as a rule someone has to remember.

  And M16, the half of the same question `sendThrough` cannot reach: WHERE A NAME IS
  LOOKED UP. A user's client hands this node a hostname, and the outbound has to turn it
  into an address before it can open anything — which, left alone, it does through the
  host's own resolver: on an entry node, inside the network the hop exists to leave, one
  query per site visited. So on a node with a hop this file carries a resolver of its own,
  and three settings put the lookup through the hop. Each is necessary and none is enough
  alone — measured against the pinned Xray, with the resolver's side watched on loopback:
    - the outbound's `settings.domainStrategy`. Without it (`AsIs`) the outbound hands the
      name to the host's resolver and the `dns` object is never consulted at all. Since the
      tenth round `routing.domainStrategy` below asks the same `dns` object one step
      earlier, so the two are belt and braces rather than one setting — and reproducing M16
      now takes removing BOTH.
    - `dns.servers`: the resolver itself, from the node's record. Never a default here.
    - `dns.tag` and the second routing rule. The resolver's own queries leave by the hop
      outbound, so they are sent from the hop's address like the traffic they are for —
      said in the file rather than left to "the first outbound is the default".
  `UseIPv4` in both places because the hop carries IPv4 only: an AAAA answer is an address
  the hop cannot reach. Xray does NOT reject a misspelt `queryStrategy` — it silently asks
  for both — so the value is held by a test, not by the checker. `clientIp` is absent on
  purpose: it would attach the asker's network to every query. And `log.dnsLog` is written
  out as `false` in EVERY config this file renders and not only in one that carries a
  resolver of its own: since the tenth round a name is resolved on every node, so every node
  has something for that key to record. With it on, every name looked up would go to the
  container log, the one record the charter says must not exist.

  And M23's ninth round, which is the same finding as the kernel tunnels' but on the other
  side of the topology: not where a user's traffic LEAVES, but WHAT THEY MAY ASK THIS
  NODE TO DIAL. `sendThrough` binds the address a connection is opened from and says
  nothing about the address it is opened to, so a user on an entry node could hand this
  proxy the leaf's own address on the hop and be answered by its SSH daemon — an RFC 1918
  address nothing on the open internet can reach, reached from the one address the leaf's
  peer entry authorises. No packet of theirs is forwarded by this node's kernel, so no
  firewall rule this project renders is anywhere on that path: the policy has to live in
  the thing that opens the connection, which is this file. The FIRST routing rule refuses
  every destination that is not on the public internet, and every second spelling of one —
  `edge.egress.NOT_THE_INTERNET`, the same list a kernel tunnel drops and never a second
  copy of it — and first is load-bearing, because Xray takes the first rule that matches
  and the rule below it matches everything from this inbound.

  Two things it deliberately does not refuse, and each would be silently broken by a wider
  rule:
    - THE RESOLVER'S OWN QUERIES. They arrive tagged `lookup` and the refusal names `in`,
      so a node record pointing at a private resolver on the far side of the hop goes on
      working — which is the whole of M16, and M16 is why the record is allowed to name a
      private one at all. Scoped by WHOSE connection it is rather than by an address and a
      port, so unlike an address exception it can never open a hole for an address a
      user's own device could hold.
    - THE HOP ITSELF. What carries this node's traffic abroad is another transport's
      interface and another process; the entry dials the leaf through the kernel and never
      through this file, so nothing here can take it away.

  It refuses by dropping rather than by answering. `blackhole` with `response.type: none`
  sends nothing to the destination and writes nothing back, which is the same choice the
  kernel rules made in preferring DROP to REJECT: an answer is a signal, and a signal is
  how a fleet is enumerated. The refusal outbound carries `sendThrough` wherever the one
  beside it does — it dials nothing, but a file in which EVERY outbound is bound to the hop
  cannot grow one that is not, and that invariant is what M3 is asserted as.

  And M23's TENTH round, which closed the two holes the ninth left open and reported. Both
  were measured against the pinned Xray run as a live proxy, not argued from the docs.

  FIRST, THE RULE IS NOT ONLY FOR A NODE WITH A HOP. It used to be rendered inside the same
  branch as `sendThrough` and the resolver, so a LEAF refused nothing at all — and a leaf's
  neighbourhood is the wider one: the far end of the hop (whose near end is the Russian
  entry), the provider's LAN, the metadata service, this node's own loopback. Every user
  this project has who is served directly by a leaf had all of it. So the refusal sits
  outside that branch now, and the only thing the exit policy still decides is WHERE what
  is carried goes — `hop`, bound to the hop's near address, or `direct` by this node's own
  route. A proxy is never one end of a hop (`edge/relay.py` refuses a transport that hands
  its consumers no address of their own), so `Egress.RELAY`'s deliberately wider licence —
  which the leaf END of a kernel-tunnel hop needs, because what arrives there is another
  NODE's traffic — can never reach this file.

  SECOND, `routing.domainStrategy: "IpOnDemand"`, which is what makes an address rule mean
  anything. Left at Xray's default (`AsIs`) the rules see the destination as the user
  sent it, so `127.0.0.1` was refused and the NAME `localhost` was reached: any name
  resolving into one of these ranges walked straight past the rule, and a wildcard DNS
  service makes such a name out of any address. `IpIfNonMatch` is a MEASURED no-op here —
  the rule below the refusal matches this inbound unconditionally, so nothing is ever left
  unmatched for it to resolve. `IpOnDemand` resolves the destination the moment a rule
  carrying addresses is read.

  AND M23's TWELFTH round, which is the same rule asked in the other family. Everything
  above held, and every range in the list was IPv4 — so the product's own rendered config,
  run unedited in the pinned Xray, refused `127.0.0.1`, refused the name `localhost` and
  refused a private v4 address, while `[::1]` answered from the node's own loopback and
  `[fc00::2]` answered from the network the node sits in. A refusal that names only one
  spelling of a destination is not a refusal.

  `routing.rules[].ip` takes both families, and `iptables` takes one, which is why the
  list `edge.egress` keeps is now split there BY DERIVATION rather than by a second list
  somebody maintains: this file takes the whole of it, and the wg family takes what its
  own tool can express. Two ranges of the registry are deliberately absent — see that
  constant, and the test that measures the mapped spelling's verdict.

  What was measured afterwards, on the same live proxy: `[::1]` and `[fc00::2]` refused,
  a globally-routable IPv6 address carried, a NAME resolving to a v6 address off the
  internet refused and one resolving to a v6 address on it carried, and the log still
  empty of every one of them.

  AND M23's THIRTEENTH round, which is the same address in a THIRD spelling. The twelfth
  round's v6 half was the registry's own, which carries the LOCAL-USE NAT64 prefix
  `64:ff9b:1::/48` and not the WELL-KNOWN one — and the well-known one is the half that
  puts an IPv4 address in its low 32 bits. So `[64:ff9b::7f00:1]` was still reached, on a
  responder bound to this node's own loopback, in the same run that refused `127.0.0.1`
  and `[::ffff:127.0.0.1]`. Measured, not inferred: the daemon does NOT fold a NAT64
  address the way it folds a mapped one — `[::ffff:192.88.99.9]` reached the host at
  `192.88.99.9` while `[64:ff9b::c058:6309]` timed out — so it is dialled as literal IPv6
  and nothing but a translator on the path turns it back.

  The list now carries the well-known prefix's EMBEDDING of each range it already refuses
  and not the prefix itself, which is the part a wider rule would have got wrong: an
  address in that prefix with a GLOBAL IPv4 address inside is how a v6-only client reaches
  the v4 internet, and this project's own scenarios use exactly such an address for the
  word "internet". Re-measured with the new list in place: `[64:ff9b::7f00:1]` refused,
  `[64:ff9b::c058:6301]` carried, and every verdict above unchanged.

  Where that resolution is asked is the part worth checking rather than assuming, because a
  lookup is the one record this project must never create and the one it most easily leaks.
  It goes to THIS FILE'S OWN `dns` object where there is one: measured, a name that only the
  node record's resolver knows is reached from a node with a hop, and the host's resolver is
  never asked — so on an entry the routing lookup crosses the hop exactly like the outbound's
  own. On a node without a hop there is no `dns` object and Xray asks the host's resolver,
  which is where that node's names have always been looked up and is abroad by construction.
  It is one lookup per NAME rather than per connection (Xray caches it for the answer's TTL,
  measured at one query for three connections), and nothing writes it down: `log.dnsLog` is
  `false`, `sniffing` is off, and a `blackhole` has nowhere to record what it refused.

  AND M34, the one place this file deliberately gives an entry node a SECOND way out. With
  `direct` (the render's `_direct_for_xray`) the file grows an outbound `direct` that is
  bound to nothing — this node's own, Russian, route — and the rules send the listed
  destinations to it. That breaks M3's "every outbound is bound to the hop" on purpose and
  only while the switch is on; with it off, or with nothing listed, not one byte below
  changes. What keeps the rest of M3 true, each measured against the pinned Xray:
    - ORDER. The refusal stays first, so a listed destination still cannot be a private
      address. Then *never direct* (names, then networks) to `hop`, then the direct names
      and networks to `direct`, then everything else to `hop`. Xray takes the first rule
      that matches, so the owner's *never* beats a source's listing, by name or by address
      — a listed name whose address is on *never* still goes through the hop, because
      `IpOnDemand` resolves it at the first rule that carries addresses.
    - NO FALLBACK. An unlisted destination still has exactly one way out, the hop; while
      the hop is down it cannot be opened, and a listed one still can.
    - LOOKUPS. A direct name is looked up at the entry-side resolver (`direct.resolver`,
      node data) and that query leaves by `direct`, matched by the resolver's ADDRESS —
      which is why the two resolvers may never be one address. `skipFallback` keeps that
      resolver from ever being asked an unlisted name. *Never direct* names are pinned to
      the hop's resolver by a `domains` list of their own: without it a never-direct name
      under a listed suffix was MEASURED being looked up at the direct resolver, while its
      traffic correctly went through the hop. Every other name is looked up exactly as M16
      left it.
    - NO EMPTY CONDITION. A rule is rendered only for a list that has something in it: a
      rule whose only condition is its inbound matches everything.
  Nothing is counted and nothing is written down: no rule here has a statistic attached,
  and `log` is unchanged.
#}
{
  "log": {
    "loglevel": "warning",
    "dnsLog": false,
    "access": "none"
  },
  "inbounds": [
    {
      "tag": "in",
      "listen": "0.0.0.0",
      "port": {{ params.port | tojson }},
      "protocol": "vless",
      "settings": {
        "clients": [
{% for client in clients %}
          {
            "id": {{ client.subject | tojson }},
            "email": {{ client.label | tojson }}
          }{{ "," if not loop.last }}
{% endfor %}
        ],
        "decryption": "none"
      },
      "streamSettings": {
        "network": "ws",
        "security": "tls",
        "wsSettings": {
          "path": {{ ws_path | tojson }}
        },
        "tlsSettings": {
          "serverName": {{ params.server_name | tojson }},
          "alpn": ["http/1.1"],
          "certificates": [
            {
              "certificateFile": {{ params.certificate_path | tojson }},
              "keyFile": {{ params.certificate_key_path | tojson }}
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      "sniffing": {
        "enabled": false
      }
    }
  ],
{% if exit_origin %}
  "dns": {
{% if direct and direct.direct_names %}
    "servers": [
{% if direct.never_names %}
      {
        "address": {{ resolver | tojson }},
        "domains": {{ direct.never_names | tojson }}
      },
{% else %}
      {{ resolver | tojson }},
{% endif %}
      {
        "address": {{ direct.resolver | tojson }},
        "domains": {{ direct.direct_names | tojson }},
        "skipFallback": true
      }
    ],
{% else %}
    "servers": [{{ resolver | tojson }}],
{% endif %}
    "queryStrategy": "UseIPv4",
    "tag": "lookup"
  },
{% endif %}
  "routing": {
    "domainStrategy": "IpOnDemand",
    "rules": [
      {
        "type": "field",
        "inboundTag": ["in"],
        "ip": [
{% for destination in refused %}
          {{ destination | tojson }}{{ "," if not loop.last }}
{% endfor %}
        ],
        "outboundTag": "refused"
      },
{% if direct %}
{% for condition, values, way in [
     ("domain", direct.never_names, "hop"),
     ("ip", direct.never_networks, "hop"),
     ("domain", direct.direct_names, "direct"),
     ("ip", direct.direct_networks, "direct"),
   ] if values %}
      {
        "type": "field",
        "inboundTag": ["in"],
        {{ condition | tojson }}: {{ values | tojson }},
        "outboundTag": {{ way | tojson }}
      },
{% endfor %}
{% endif %}
      {
        "type": "field",
        "inboundTag": ["in"],
        "outboundTag": {{ ("hop" if exit_origin else "direct") | tojson }}
{% if exit_origin %}
      },
{% if direct %}
      {
        "type": "field",
        "inboundTag": ["lookup"],
        "ip": [{{ direct.resolver | tojson }}],
        "outboundTag": "direct"
      },
{% endif %}
      {
        "type": "field",
        "inboundTag": ["lookup"],
        "outboundTag": "hop"
      }
{% else %}
      }
{% endif %}
    ]
  },
  "outbounds": [
    {
{% if exit_origin %}
      "tag": "hop",
      "protocol": "freedom",
      "sendThrough": {{ exit_origin | tojson }},
      "settings": {
        "domainStrategy": "UseIPv4"
      }
{% else %}
      "tag": "direct",
      "protocol": "freedom"
{% endif %}
    },
{% if direct %}
    {
      "tag": "direct",
      "protocol": "freedom",
      "settings": {
        "domainStrategy": "UseIPv4"
      }
    },
{% endif %}
    {
      "tag": "refused",
      "protocol": "blackhole",
{% if exit_origin %}
      "sendThrough": {{ exit_origin | tojson }},
{% endif %}
      "settings": {
        "response": {
          "type": "none"
        }
      }
    }
  ]
}
